Mouse control in edit mode:
    Left down: paint currently selected type. Dragging around
	should then paint more of it, with the same owner.
    Right down: select entity. Dragging should then force it around, and
	scroll wheel rotate it, and left click delete it. Unselected on right
	button up. If blocked forces mean the cursor ends up no longer on the
	piece, so be it - don't warp the cursor or release the piece.
    Scroll wheel: adjust currently selected tile type.

So abstractly, we have the following new commands (which should have keyboard
triggers too):
    place currently selected tile type - the owner is recorded;
    terp rotation commands when nothing selected as adjusting paint selection.

(so selected tile is of type (Maybe PieceIdx, Tile))

Mouse processing generates commands in edit mode as follows:
    If cursor is out of bounds: unselect.

    Else:
	If right button was but isn't down, unselect.

	Generate a shortest path of movement commands from previously selected hex
	to currently moused-over hex; if left button was and is down, intersperse
	paint commands.

	If left button is but wasn't down, paint.

	If right button is but wasn't down, select.

    Handle scroll wheel, generating rotation or next/prev selection commands.

Mouse control in play mode:
    Don't use click-and-drag - too easy to make mistakes.

    Instead: click on tool to select; click adjacent to selected tool to move; 
    mouse wheel for rotation.


